---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ConceptPageHero from '../../components/concepts/ConceptPageHero.astro';
import ConceptPager from '../../components/concepts/ConceptPager.astro';
import { getConcept } from '../../lib/concepts';

const c = getConcept('frequency');
---

<Layout title={`${c.title} | Identity Engineering`}>
	<main class="min-h-screen">
		<ConceptPageHero
			scene={c.scene}
			tag={`${c.tag} · ${c.layerLabel}`}
			title={c.title}
			lede={c.blurb}
			scrollHint={c.scrollHint}
		/>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Definition</div>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white md:text-4xl">
					The dynamic state of the tension continuum.
				</h2>
				<p>
					<strong class="text-white/80">Frequency</strong> is the engineering descriptor of the high-dimensional tension continuum in which identities exist and interact. It captures the spectrum of characteristic oscillation frequencies, resonance conditions and damping ratios that determine how an identity’s internal tension field evolves over time.
				</p>
				<p>
					At its core, every identity is defined by a fundamental tension: the ongoing negotiation between <strong class="text-white/80">Sameness</strong> (coherence, continuity of the stem) and <strong class="text-white/80">Causal Entropy</strong> (opening of future possibilities). Frequency makes this tension visible as a dynamic, multidimensional field rather than a simple binary opposition.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<div class="mb-6 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">The Inner Tension Field</div>
				<p class="text-lg text-white/55 mb-6">
					The tension an identity experiences is not a single force pulling in one direction. It is a high-dimensional field generated by multiple, often conflicting demands:
				</p>
				<ul class="space-y-4 text-lg text-white/55">
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Sameness vs. Entropy:</strong> the foundational tension between preserving coherence and expanding possibilities.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Internal vs. External:</strong> tension arising from one’s own vision versus external expectations, relationships, and regulatory forces.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Stability vs. Expansion:</strong> the tension between maintaining current structure and the drive to grow or differentiate.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Resonance vs. Dissonance:</strong> tension created by alignment or misalignment with other identities and idea-fields.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p class="mt-6 text-lg text-white/55">
					Frequency is the tool that makes this multidimensional tension field describable and, to a certain degree, steerable.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<div class="mb-6 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Mechanics</div>
				<ul class="space-y-6 text-lg text-white/55">
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Tension field:</strong> The high-dimensional continuum of internal stress, deflection and competing demands an identity is permanently exposed to.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Frequency spectrum:</strong> The characteristic rates and patterns at which tension oscillates. These patterns reveal which tensions are dominant, which are amplifying, and which are decaying.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Resonance:</strong> When frequencies of different tension fields align, mutual amplification occurs. This can be highly productive (shared vision, strong relationships) or destructive (reinforcing dysfunctional patterns). The full mechanics are developed below.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Damping (general):</strong> The rate at which tension oscillations lose energy. Damping is a fundamental parameter of any tension field. Its higher-order, regulatory application at collective scale is developed in the Collective Identity layer (see Damping).
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Deepening · Resonance</div>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white md:text-4xl">
					Efficient coupling of tension fields
				</h2>
				<p>
					<strong class="text-white/80">Resonance</strong> is the condition under which characteristic frequencies of different tension fields align so that mutual amplification occurs. It is the primary coupling mechanism of Frequency: the way energy and coherence transfer between fields with minimal dissipative loss.
				</p>
				<p>
					No energy is created. Energy is transferred and focused. The driving source (an external field, another identity’s Mass and Curvature, or the identity’s own Vision Gradient) supplies the energy. Matching frequencies reduce the fraction of that energy that dissipates as internal friction, heat, or incoherent oscillation. The remainder is available for coherent motion along the Stem.
				</p>

				<div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30 p-6">
					<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-white/40 mb-3">Working definition</div>
					<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
						Resonance is constructive interference between oscillatory modes of tension fields. When the phase and frequency of σ<sub>ext</sub>, the narrative frequency of σ<sub>past</sub>, and σ<sub>future</sub> align, the amplitude of the shared mode rises. The result is higher effective force along the Stem for the same input energy.
					</p>
				</div>

				<p>
					This is why identities seek matching frequencies. Alignment lowers damping mismatch. Lower dissipative loss increases the fraction of available tension energy that can be converted into coherent agency. In the framework’s working model of effective mass,
				</p>
				<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-6 font-mono text-sm text-violet-200/90">
					<span class="inline-block whitespace-nowrap">M<sub>eff</sub> ∝ depth × ownership × consistency</span>
				</div>
				<p>
					Resonance raises the <strong class="text-white/80">consistency</strong> term. Shared phase, shared narrative frequency, and aligned future force make ownership denser and more defensible. The density increase is mutual when two or more fields lock: each experiences the other as a constructive contribution to σ<sub>ext</sub> rather than as pure interference. Internally this mutual density effect has been referred to as <strong class="text-white/80">Ber</strong>: not a physical transfer of mass, but a resonant coupling that raises effective Mass density on both sides through reduced dissipation and higher consistency.
				</p>

				<div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
					<div class="rounded-2xl border border-cyan-400/20 bg-cyan-400/[0.04] p-5">
						<div class="mb-2 text-xs uppercase tracking-[3px] text-cyan-300/80">Productive resonance</div>
						<p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/70">
							Shared vision, coherent relationships, idea-fields that amplify Causal Entropy. The common mode expands option value while preserving Stem recognizability.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="rounded-2xl border border-violet-400/20 bg-violet-400/[0.04] p-5">
						<div class="mb-2 text-xs uppercase tracking-[3px] text-violet-300/80">Destructive resonance</div>
						<p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/70">
							Reinforcing dysfunctional loops, chronic mismatch that locks energy into high-dissipation modes, or forced frequency coupling that flattens Mass on one side.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<p>
					Resonance is therefore the bridge between Frequency (local sampling of the tension continuum), Gravitation (attraction of high-Mass vision), and the mutual density effect (Ber). It does not invent energy or mass. It optimizes the conversion of existing tension energy into coherent Stem force and raises the consistency component of effective Mass.
				</p>

				<div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30 p-6">
					<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-white/40 mb-3">Engineering levers</div>
					<ul class="space-y-3 text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
						<li>Diagnose which external fields currently lock into constructive vs destructive modes with your own spectrum.</li>
						<li>Sharpen the narrative frequency of σ<sub>past</sub> so that constructive interference with σ<sub>future</sub> becomes possible.</li>
						<li>Seek or design frequency matches that raise consistency without collapsing ownership or jurisdiction over the Stem.</li>
						<li>Apply targeted damping where over-amplification of a destructive mode is already visible.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>

				<div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30 p-6">
					<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-white/40 mb-3">Resonance probes</div>
					<ul class="space-y-3 text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
						<li>Which external tension fields currently produce constructive interference with my Stem force, and which produce net dissipation?</li>
						<li>Where is my narrative frequency of σ<sub>past</sub> still out of phase with σ<sub>future</sub>?</li>
						<li>What single Owned Act would raise consistency (and therefore M<sub>eff</sub>) through cleaner frequency alignment without surrendering jurisdiction over the Stem?</li>
						<li>Am I seeking resonance for higher Causal Entropy, or for temporary relief from internal friction?</li>
					</ul>
				</div>

				<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/45">
					<strong class="text-white/65">Status:</strong> working definition and inspirational formalization. Resonance is treated as a core mechanical aspect of Frequency, not as a separate Core Concept. Collective-scale frequency coupling and mismatch remain under-specified (see Critical Gaps on scaling and competing curvatures).
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<div class="mb-6 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Rotation and Tension</div>
				<p class="text-lg text-white/55 mb-6">
					Rotation has a double relationship to tension:
				</p>
				<ul class="space-y-4 text-lg text-white/55">
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Stabilizing effect:</strong> Strong rotation around the Identity Stem increases resistance to small and medium disturbances (gyroscopic effect). This allows an identity to hold significantly more internal tension without losing its fundamental orientation.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Tension-generating effect:</strong> Rotation simultaneously creates its own centrifugal tension: a drive toward expansion, differentiation and, in extreme cases, fragmentation. This self-generated tension must be absorbed by sufficient Mass and internal cohesion.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p class="mt-6 text-lg text-white/55">
					Healthy high rotation therefore requires enough Mass to “hold” the centrifugal tension it produces. Without sufficient Mass, strong rotation shifts from stabilizing to fragmenting.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Deepening · The Now</div>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white md:text-4xl">
					The tension field as stress tensor
				</h2>
				<p>
					In the singular point of the Now the high-dimensional tension continuum is experienced as a local stress field. In continuum-mechanics language this is a stress tensor: the set of internal forces that act on every surface element of the identity’s configuration space.
				</p>
				<p>
					We write the effective local tensor as the superposition of three primary components:
				</p>
				<ul class="space-y-4">
					<li class="border-l border-cyan-400/30 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">External component</strong> (σ<sub>ext</sub>): forces arising from the curvature and gravitation of surrounding masses (other identities, structures, events). The identity samples these forces through its frequency bandwidth.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-cyan-400/30 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Past / trajectory component</strong> (σ<sub>past</sub>): the inertial and phase contribution of the owned history. This is not the raw State Differential alone; it is the differential modulated by the <em class="text-white/70 not-italic">narrative frequency</em> of the trajectory: the coherent, owned interpretation that actually exerts force in the Now.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-violet-400/30 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Future component</strong> (σ<sub>future</sub>): the oscillatory expression of the Vision Gradient / future force term. In the force-balance formalization of Time this appears as the directed push T<sub>F</sub> · ∇O<sub>τ</sub>. In the Now it is experienced as a vibrating directional tension that biases local decisions toward higher option value.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					The net local force that results from this tensor is the same object that appears in the Time formalization as the stem force:
				</p>
				<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-6 font-mono text-sm text-violet-200/90">
					<span class="inline-block whitespace-nowrap">F<sub>stem</sub> = T<sub>F</sub> · ∇O<sub>τ</sub>  −  λ · ∇D<sub>id</sub></span>
				</div>
				<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/45">
					<strong class="text-white/65">In plain words:</strong> the total stem force is the future-opening push minus the identity-protection pullback. Frequency describes the oscillatory and resonant dynamics through which this force balance is actually lived in the Now.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Decision</div>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white md:text-4xl">
					Local modulation of the tensor
				</h2>
				<p>
					Decision is the capacity to intervene in σ_total / F_stem at the singular point of the Now. It is not an abstract free will floating above the geometry; it is the Architect-level act of reorienting or re-weighting the components of the tension field so that the resulting Owned Act advances the stem.
				</p>
				<p>
					The primary engineering levers remain those already stated in Time: make the historical trajectory explicit enough for a meaningful identity-deviation term, and sharpen future option value so the vision force is not vague. Frequency supplies the additional instruments of resonance and damping that determine how cleanly those levers transmit into the local tensor.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Narrative</div>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white md:text-4xl">
					The frequency of the trajectory
				</h2>
				<p>
					The mathematical State Differential (δx = dx/dt) is the observed history. The <strong class="text-white/80">narrative of one’s own story</strong> is the active, owned modulation of that history into a coherent oscillatory component of the tension field. It sets phase and amplitude of σ_past.
				</p>
				<p>
					A coherent, empowering narrative produces constructive interference with the future component and stabilizes the rotation of the stem (lower wobble). A dissonant narrative increases internal friction and reduces the effective bandwidth available for decision. The narrative is therefore not decorative; it is a force-bearing frequency layer of the past that acts in the Now.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<div class="mb-6 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Embodied Mechanisms</div>
				<p>
					At human scale the tension continuum is concretely instantiated through wave phenomena. External inputs arrive as frequency-encoded carriers: acoustic waves (sound, 20 Hz to 20 kHz) and electromagnetic waves (visible light) that directly perturb and inform the internal tension field. Internally, cognitive and affective processes emerge from neural oscillatory dynamics across characteristic bands; these oscillations constitute the biological substrate of the tension spectrum itself.
				</p>
				<p>
					Resonance and damping thereby become directly experienceable and engineerable: alignment between external frequency signatures and internal oscillatory modes amplifies coherent evolution of the tension field and expands causal entropy; chronic mismatch or dissonant overload increases energetic dissipation and drift.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<p>
					<strong class="text-white/80">Engineering work:</strong> Develop the capacity to read the current tension field of an identity (including its own generated tensions through rotation and mass creation) and to intervene deliberately: shaping resonance, applying targeted damping, aligning internal cycles with supportive external fields, and using Questions as Probes to measure the relative weight of the three tensor components in the current Now.
				</p>
				<div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30 p-6">
					<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-white/40 mb-3">Practical prompts</div>
					<ul class="space-y-3 text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
						<li>Which cycles in my week are constructive resonance, and which are destructive loops?</li>
						<li>Where do I need more damping (over-amplification)?</li>
						<li>Where do I need less damping (suppressed momentum)?</li>
						<li>Which external frequency inputs (sound, light, rhythm) currently stabilize or destabilize my tension field?</li>
						<li>Which of the three tensor components currently dominates my Now: external, narrative-past, or future force?</li>
						<li>What single Owned Act would most cleanly reorient the local tension field toward higher option value?</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-cyan-400/20 bg-cyan-400/[0.04] p-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-white/60">
					<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-cyan-300/80">Foundations anchor</div>
					<p>
						The treatment of a complex tension continuum as a spectrum of independent modes that can be diagnosed, amplified or damped is rooted in Fourier’s harmonic analysis.
						See <a href="/foundations/fourier-frequency-spectrum-and-harmonic-analysis" class="text-white/80 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50">Joseph Fourier: Frequency Spectrum and Harmonic Analysis</a>.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<ConceptPager id="frequency" />
	</main>
</Layout>
